{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nAdvisory from S6 (6f14) LLM code review (Gerrit 1850). `OpcertServiceConfig.from_env`\n(`src/rebar/opcert_service/config.py:53`) reads the inline-PEM key source through\n`_private_key_env()` (`config.py:76`, `os.environ.get(\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\")` at\n`config.py:79`) and copies it into the config dataclass, but never removes it from\n`os.environ`. Confirmed on base `b91f8f6830`: with the variable set, `from_env()` returns the\nvalue as `cfg.private_key` AND `\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\" in os.environ` is still True\nafterwards, and a child `subprocess.run` sees it too. The service does spawn children after\nconfig load — `keyprov._validate_key_file` runs `ssh-keygen` (`keyprov.py:135`) and\n`workspace._git` runs `git` (`workspace.py:73`), neither passing an explicit `env=`, so both\ninherit the raw PEM today.\n\nThe DEPLOYED path sets `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH` only (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:285`;\n`REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY` appears nowhere under `infra/`), so this is hygiene on the\nsecondary dev/compat inline-PEM path, not a fix for a live exposure. The person it helps is\na developer or CI operator running the op-cert service from an inline PEM, whose key would\notherwise sit in the process environment for the process lifetime.\n\n## Approach\n\nConsume-on-read at the single env boundary: `_private_key_env()` captures the value and then\npops the variable (`os.environ.pop(\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\", None)`), so `from_env`\nTRANSFERS the secret into the config object instead of copying it. `compose_signer` already\nreads `cfg.private_key` via `_read_source_key` (`keyprov.py:79`) and never touches the\nenvironment, so nothing downstream of the config object changes.\n\nThat seam is right because `from_env` is the ONLY reader of the variable in the tree\n(`git grep REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY src/` hits `config.py:79` plus docstrings in `config.py`\nand `keyprov.py`), and it is invoked exactly once, at module scope, by its single caller\n`src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py:171` (`app.state.config = OpcertServiceConfig.from_env()`).\nPopping inside `compose_signer` instead would fire an environment side effect on a config the\ncaller built by hand (which every existing test does); popping in `app.py`'s lifespan would\nleave the variable live across the whole import-to-startup window.\n\nConfig-reload semantics: there is no reload path to break. No other call site of\n`OpcertServiceConfig.from_env` exists under `src/`, `tests/`, `scripts/`, or `infra/`, and the\ncontainer runs `uvicorn rebar.opcert_service.app:app` with neither `--workers` nor `--reload`\n(`infra/compose/Dockerfile.opcert:47`), so the module is imported once per process. To keep\nthat a checked fact rather than an assumption, the change is covered by a test that calls\n`from_env()` TWICE and pins the consume-once contract, so a future reload path fails loudly in\ntests instead of silently losing the key.\n\nWhat the hardening buys, precisely:\n\n- It removes the raw PEM from any in-process `os.environ` dump taken after config load — a\n  crash handler, an error reporter, a debug endpoint, a traceback that renders the environment.\n- It removes it from the environment inherited by children spawned after config load\n  (`ssh-keygen`, `git`), which do inherit it today.\n\nWhat it does NOT buy — stated so the ticket does not overclaim:\n\n- It does NOT remove the key from process memory. `cfg.private_key` stays reachable for the\n  process lifetime through `app.state.config`, and `compose_signer` writes a 0600 copy to a\n  process-owned directory. A core dump or heap scrape still exposes it.\n- It does NOT help against anything that already read the variable, nor against the deployment\n  platform's own record of the injected value.\n- It does NOT scrub `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux, which reflects the exec-time environment\n  block that `unsetenv` does not rewrite.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/opcert_service/config.py` — `_private_key_env()` pops after capturing; its\n  docstring and the `private_key` field comment (`config.py:47`) state the consume-once\n  contract.\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_privkey_env_pop_932d.py` (new) — the tests below.\n- `docs/env-vars.md` — regenerated by `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py`, not hand-edited.\n  The generator records the line of `_str_env`'s non-literal read, which the new docstring\n  shifts; a stale file fails the CI drift gate asserted by\n  `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_http_transport.py::test_generators_not_stale_and_include_new_keys`.\n  Relatedly, the read in `_private_key_env` STAYS an `os.environ.get` with a literal key\n  (the pop is a separate statement after it), because the generator recognizes only\n  `os.environ[\"X\"]` / `os.environ.get(\"X\")` / `os.getenv(\"X\")` / the `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS`\n  (`scripts/gen_env_registry.py:12`, `:37`) — folding the read into the pop would silently\n  drop `REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY` from the registry.\n\nOut of scope: `keyprov.py`, `app.py`, the `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH` file source, and any broader\ncredential-hygiene work. Any further weakness found while working is filed as its own ticket\nrather than absorbed here.\n\n## Testing\n\nNew `tests/unit/test_opcert_privkey_env_pop_932d.py`. Secrets in fixtures are either an\nobvious dummy string (`\"DUMMY-NOT-A-REAL-KEY\"`) or a throwaway key generated by `ssh-keygen`\ninto `tmp_path` — never a plausible-looking committed PEM.\n\n- Happy path (`test_from_env_consumes_inline_key`): with the variable set to the dummy,\n  `from_env()` returns it as `cfg.private_key` AND `\"REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY\" not in\n  os.environ` afterwards. This is the RED test: on unmodified `main` it fails on the second\n  assertion — the variable is still present — which is exactly the defect.\n- Reload path (`test_second_from_env_call_has_no_inline_key`): a SECOND `from_env()` call in\n  the same process returns `private_key is None` and does not raise, pinning the consume-once\n  contract so a future re-read cannot regress silently.\n- Compose still works (`test_compose_still_works_after_pop`): generate a real throwaway\n  Ed25519 key into `tmp_path`, set its text as the inline variable, and assert\n  `compose_signer(OpcertServiceConfig.from_env())` returns a signer whose 0600 copy exists —\n  proving the pop happens after the value is captured, not before.\n- Edge, unset (`test_unset_is_noop`): with the variable absent, `from_env()` returns\n  `private_key is None` and raises no `KeyError`.\n- Non-interference (`test_key_path_source_untouched`): with `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH` set and\n  the inline variable unset, `from_env().key_path` resolves and `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH`\n  REMAINS in `os.environ` — only the inline secret is consumed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `OpcertServiceConfig.from_env()` returns the inline PEM as `cfg.private_key` and leaves\n      `REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY` absent from `os.environ`; checked by `pytest\n      tests/unit/test_opcert_privkey_env_pop_932d.py::test_from_env_consumes_inline_key`.\n- [x] A second `from_env()` call in the same process yields `private_key is None` and does not\n      raise, pinning consume-once reload semantics; checked by\n      `::test_second_from_env_call_has_no_inline_key`.\n- [x] `compose_signer` still composes a working signer from the inline source after the pop,\n      on a real throwaway Ed25519 key; checked by `::test_compose_still_works_after_pop`.\n- [x] With the variable unset the pop is a no-op, and the `REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH` source is\n      unaffected (that variable stays in `os.environ`); checked by `::test_unset_is_noop` and\n      `::test_key_path_source_untouched`.\n- [x] `docs/env-vars.md` is regenerated and current: `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py\n      --check` exits 0 and `REBAR_OPCERT_PRIVATE_KEY` is still listed in it.\n- [x] Gates are green on the change: `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, and\n      `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` reports `new=0 increased=0\n      stale=0`; the whole `tests/` suite passes (not just `tests/unit`)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787200327212278001,"uuid":"b9b433ed-dc76-4b80-a096-7fad509f6a4f"}